\description{
Computes the five rates in \code{\link{cacs_acs_default_rates}}, such as the
poverty rate, for each site and drive-time pair in the output of
\code{\link[=cacs_propagate_moe]{cacs_propagate_moe()}}, each with a margin of error (MOE). A margin of
error is the half-width of a confidence interval. The rates are added
after the input rows, as five new rows for each pair.
}
\details{
Each rate is the ratio of two weighted counts of ACS estimates. The two
counts and their variances are read from the \code{cacs_aggregation_carriers}
attribute that \code{\link[=cacs_intersect_weight]{cacs_intersect_weight()}} attaches. The margin of error is
computed from them at the confidence level that \code{\link[=cacs_propagate_moe]{cacs_propagate_moe()}}
records in the \code{cacs_confidence_level} attribute (the 90 percent level if
there is none). The result does not keep \code{cacs_aggregation_carriers}, so
\code{cacs_derive_rates()} gives an error if it is run on its own result.

The numerator of each of the five rates is part of its denominator, so
the rates are proportions, for which the Census Bureau's handbook gives
the proportion formula, C1 (U.S. Census Bureau 2020, chapter 8). By
default, the margin of error of every rate is computed with the ratio
formula, C2, which for the same rate is never narrower than the margin the
proportion formula gives. \code{formula_dispatch} can choose the proportion
formula for two of the five rates. The table gives the formula used for
each rate with each value of \code{formula_dispatch}.\tabular{llll}{
   Rate \tab Default \tab \code{"auto"} \tab \code{"proportion_subset"} \cr
   \code{poverty_rate} \tab ratio \tab proportion \tab proportion \cr
   \code{labor_force_participation} \tab ratio \tab proportion \tab proportion \cr
   \code{snap_rate} \tab ratio \tab ratio \tab ratio \cr
   \code{ssi_rate} \tab ratio \tab ratio \tab ratio \cr
   \code{unemp_rate} \tab ratio \tab ratio \tab ratio \cr
}


\code{"auto"} and \code{"proportion_subset"} give the same rates and margins of
error. \code{"proportion_subset"} also gives a warning naming the three rates
that keep the ratio formula, and lists them in the
\code{formula_downgraded_rates} element of the \code{cacs_rate_provenance}
attribute, a list recording how the rates were computed. The warning calls
those three rates ineligible for the proportion formula. That wording
describes the package's list of rates and not the data: the numerator of
each of the three is part of its denominator. Both formulas are given in
the "MOE formula families" section of \code{\link[=cacs_propagate_moe]{cacs_propagate_moe()}}.

Under \code{"auto"} and \code{"proportion_subset"}, the three rates in the table keep
the ratio formula whatever the values are; the choice is built into the
package and does not depend on the data. \code{unemp_rate} keeps it to
reproduce the 2025 analysis the package was first written for, and the
package gives no reason for \code{snap_rate} and \code{ssi_rate}.
\code{vignette("theory-derived-rates", package = "catchmentACS")} describes how
the proportion formula can be computed for them from the \code{estimate} and
\code{moe} of the rows of their two ACS codes.

When the value under the square root of the proportion formula is
negative, the row uses the ratio formula instead (see
\code{\link[=cacs_propagate_moe]{cacs_propagate_moe()}}), with \code{moe_fallback = TRUE} and
\code{moe_fallback_reason = "negative_variance"}.
}
\section{Rates that are NA}{

A rate and its margin of error are \code{NA} when the numerator or the
denominator, or the margin of error of either, is missing for the pair.
This happens when a code of the rate has no rows in the ACS data, when a
tract in the area has a missing estimate or margin of error for one of
the two codes (including a Census Bureau code that
\code{\link[=cacs_intersect_weight]{cacs_intersect_weight()}} sets to \code{NA}), and when no tract is left for the
pair. The codes of all
five rates are in \code{\link{cacs_acs_default_vars}}, the default variables of
\code{\link[=cacs_acs_prefetch]{cacs_acs_prefetch()}} and \code{\link[=cacs_run]{cacs_run()}}. Rates that are \code{NA} for these
reasons have \code{"carrier"} in \code{failure_origin}, the column that records the
step at which a row failed; the value is named after the
\code{cacs_aggregation_carriers} attribute, which holds the weighted totals. A
rate whose denominator is zero is also \code{NA}, as is its margin of error,
with \code{"zero_denominator"} in \code{moe_fallback_reason} and \code{TRUE} in
\code{moe_fallback}.

One warning at the end counts these rows and the rows where the ratio
formula replaced the proportion formula. Its class is
\code{catchmentACS_warning_runtime}; when it counts only rows with
\code{failure_origin = "carrier"}, it also has the class
\code{catchmentACS_warning_carrier_missing}.
}

\section{Tract counts for rates}{

On rate rows, \code{n_tracts_num} and \code{n_tracts_den} give the numbers of
tracts combined for the numerator and for the denominator (the values of
\code{n_tracts} on the rows of those two ACS codes), and \code{n_tracts} is \code{NA}.
On the rows of the ACS variables, \code{n_tracts_num} and \code{n_tracts_den} are
\code{NA}, and they are also \code{NA} on rate rows with
\code{failure_origin = "carrier"}.

When, in the ACS data, a tract in the area has a row for one of the two
codes of a rate and not for the other, for example after rows with a
missing estimate have been removed, the numerator and the denominator are
totals over different sets of tracts. The rate is then no longer the
share of one population: it can be much larger or smaller than the share
for the area, and even above 1, and no warning is given. The two counts
then differ unless the two codes lack the same number of tracts, so equal
counts do not rule this out. Rows whose estimate is missing, kept as \code{NA}
rows rather than removed, make the rate \code{NA}, with a warning (see the
"Rates that are NA" section). \code{\link[=cacs_acs_validate]{cacs_acs_validate()}} shows how to check
that every tract has one row for each variable.
}

\section{Checking rates against ranges}{

With \code{options(catchmentACS.audit_rates = TRUE)}, each rate is compared
with a fixed range: 0 to 0.6 for \code{poverty_rate}, 0 to 0.5 for
\code{snap_rate}, 0 to 0.15 for \code{ssi_rate}, 0 to 0.3 for \code{unemp_rate}, and
0.3 to 0.85 for \code{labor_force_participation}. For each rate with values
outside its range, a warning of class
\code{catchmentACS_warning_rate_out_of_range} gives the number of those rows
and the smallest and largest of their values; the rates are not changed.
The \code{cacs_rate_audit} attribute records whether the check was on
(\code{enabled}), the number of rate rows outside the ranges
(\code{n_out_of_bounds}), and those rows (\code{rows}). It is attached even when
the check is off (the default), with \code{enabled = FALSE} and no rows.
}

\examples{
# Turn the cache off while this example runs (see ?cacs_set_cache).
old <- options(catchmentACS.cache_enabled = FALSE)

# Example data bundled with the package: the drive-time areas are circles
# with a radius of 1 km per minute, and the ACS data are made up.
library(sf)
iso <- readRDS(system.file("extdata", "legacy_2025_isochrones.rds",
                           package = "catchmentACS"))
acs <- readRDS(system.file("extdata", "sample_alabama_subset.rds",
                           package = "catchmentACS"))
iso_07 <- iso[iso$site_id == "AL_SITE_07" & iso$drive_time_min == 10, ]
agg <- cacs_intersect_weight(iso_sf = iso_07, acs_sf = acs,
                             verbose = FALSE)
prop <- cacs_propagate_moe(agg, verbose = FALSE)

# The five rates, with margins of error at the 90 percent level
out <- cacs_derive_rates(prop, verbose = FALSE)
is_rate <- out$estimand_family == "derived_rate"
out[is_rate, c("variable", "estimate", "moe", "moe_formula_effective")]

# Margins of error with the default and with formula_dispatch = "auto"
out_auto <- cacs_derive_rates(prop, formula_dispatch = "auto",
                              verbose = FALSE)
tibble::tibble(variable = out$variable[is_rate],
               moe_default = out$moe[is_rate],
               moe_auto = out_auto$moe[is_rate],
               formula_auto = out_auto$moe_formula_effective[is_rate])

options(old)
}
